{
  "description": "Mixed-integer linear programming model for integrated train rescheduling and speed management under partial segment blockage. Sets: T trains, S stations, L track segments (bidirectional). Parameters: original arrival a0[t,s], departure d0[t,s]; travel time windows tau_min[t,l], tau_max[t,l]; energy cost per unit time e[t]; disruption window [T_start,T_end] on segment l* (blocked side). Binary x[t,l] = 1 if train t uses original direction on segment l, 0 if rerouted to opposite track. Continuous arrival A[t,s], departure D[t,s]; segment travel time y[t,l]; speed level v[t,l] (discretized). Objective: minimize weighted sum of total delay and energy consumption: minimize sum_t sum_s (A[t,s]-a0[t,s]) + alpha * sum_t sum_l e[t]*y[t,l]. Constraints: 1) Timing consistency: D[t,s]=A[t,s]+dwell[s]; A[t,s+1]=D[t,s]+y[t,seg(t,s)]. 2) Travel time bounds: tau_min[t,l] <= y[t,l] <= tau_max[t,l]. 3) Disruption feasibility: if segment l* blocked side, then x[t,l*]=0 for trains originally using blocked side; rerouted trains must satisfy y[t,l*] >= T_start - D[t,prev] and <= T_end - A[t,next]. 4) Conflict avoidance on shared track: for any two trains i,j using same direction on same segment l, enforce ordering: either A_i_exit <= A_j_entry or vice versa using binary order variable o[i,j,l]. 5) Capacity: at most one train on a segment at a time: sum_t x[t,l] <=1 for opposite direction conflict. 6) Speed-energy coupling: y[t,l] = length[l]/v[t,l]; v[t,l] in discretized set; energy = e[t]*y[t,l]. 7) Binary variables x[t,l] and o[i,j,l]. Assumptions: dwell times known, segment lengths given, speed levels linearized.\n"
}

