  "description": "Generate a MILP model for integrated train rescheduling and speed management under partial segment blockage. Variables: binary route choice r_{i,s} for train i using side s (0/1), binary order o_{i,j} if train i precedes j on shared track, continuous arrival times a_{i,k} at station k, departure times d_{i,k}, travel times t_{i,k} between stations, speed levels v_{i,k} (continuous) within bounds. Objective: minimize weighted sum of total delay Σ_i (a_{i,last}- scheduled_i) and energy consumption Σ_i Σ_k (α v_{i,k}^2 + β v_{i,k}) * t_{i,k}. Constraints: (1) each train chooses exactly one side in disruption area: Σ_s r_{i,s}=1 for trains crossing disrupted segment. (2) precedence constraints on shared track: if o_{i,j}=1 then d_{i,exit}+ safety ≤ a_{j,entry}. (3) timing consistency: a_{i,k}=d_{i,k-1}+ t_{i,k-1}. (4) travel time linked to speed: t_{i,k}= distance_{k}/v_{i,k}. (5) speed bounds: v_min ≤ v_{i,k} ≤ v_max. (6) dwell time limits: dwell_min ≤ d_{i,k}-a_{i,k} ≤ dwell_max. (7) disruption window: for trains using blocked side, cannot occupy segment during [T_start,T_end]; enforce a_{i,entry} ≥ T_end or d_{i,exit} ≤ T_start via big-M with r_{i,blocked}. (8) binary order variables linearized with big-M. (9) cancellation option: binary y_i, if y_i=1 train cancelled, then no timing constraints, add penalty M_c*y_i in objective. Provide full formulation with sets I trains, S sides, K stations, parameters. Assumptions: single line with two sides, disruption affects one side, energy model quadratic approximated linear via piecewise linearization (use α,β)."
}

{
  "count": 1,
  "description": "Integrated train rescheduling and speed management problem (TRSMP) for high-speed railway under partial segment blockage. Variables: binary route choice (original or opposite track), binary ordering for conflicting trains, continuous arrival/departure times at stations, continuous speed levels per track segment. Objective: minimize weighted sum of total delay and total energy consumption. Constraints: (1) each train must choose a feasible route; (2) flow conservation of train through stations; (3) time feasibility linking speeds to travel times (t = length / speed); (4) safety headway constraints between any two trains on same track segment (arrival time difference >= headway); (5) bidirectional conflict avoidance on shared track during disruption window; (6) dwell time bounds; (7) speed limits per segment; (8) binary ordering variables enforce which train passes first on a conflicting segment; (9) energy consumption linearized as a function of speed (e.g., energy = a*speed + b)."
}

